    "content_for_agents": {
      "summary": "The Lesser Key of Solomon (Lemegeton) is a 17th-century compilation attributed to King Solomon. It contains five books: the Ars Goetia (72 demons), Ars Theurgia-Goetia (aerial spirits), Ars Paulina (planetary angels), Ars Almadel (wax tablet angels), and Ars Notoria (prayers for memory and knowledge). Mathers' 1904 translation remains the standard English version.",
      "key_concepts": [
        "The 72 demons of the Goetia with their seals",
        "Planetary hour system",
        "The Almadel as a wax tablet ritual tool",
        "Ars Notoria prayers for memory and knowledge",
        "The hierarchy of spirits under the 72"
      ],
      "historical_context": "The Lemegeton is a 17th-century compilation that drew from earlier sources including the Pseudomonarchia Daemonum and Agrippa's Three Books of Occult Philosophy. It became the most influential grimoire of the ceremonial magic tradition.",
      "modern_relevance": "The Goetia's 72 demons are the most recognized set of spirits in Western occultism. The Almadel system is still practiced by ceremonial magicians. The Ars Notoria's memory prayers influenced later memory techniques.",
